description:
## Outcome
deploy/koderbot/ holds the files the owner needs to build an isolated runner container:
- the LXD profile;
- a provisioning script that installs a root-owned toolchain and runner;
- a probe that fails on any isolation breach and refuses a memory test without a limit;
- a job-started hook that refuses any job other than DnaCalc/DnaCalc's koderbot.yml on master;
- OWNER_STEPS.md.

AGENTS.md gains the owner-approved rules this route needs.

## Context
Library: Infrastructure (deploy/koderbot, AGENTS.md).

Host facts:
- 16 vCPU, 61 GiB RAM, no swap.
- Caddy admin on 127.0.0.1:2019; koderbot services on 18789, 18790 and 8765.
- koderbot has NOPASSWD sudo and lxd membership, and agents reach it over ssh.
- LXD is not initialised.

GitHub Free: no rulesets, no environments, 2 seats, no 2FA requirement. A repository runner cannot be limited to one workflow.

This bead builds no workflow; K1b adds koderbot.yml after the owner registers the runner.

Part of epic dc-gh6q; held by dc-oy6l until the owner has ruled on the plan's decisions. Plan keys used in this text: W1 = dc-gh6q.1, K1a = dc-gh6q.2, R1 = dc-gh6q.3, K1b = dc-gh6q.4, K2 = dc-gh6q.5, K3 = dc-gh6q.6, K4 = dc-gh6q.7, S1 = dc-gh6q.8, S2 = dc-gh6q.9, S3 = dc-gh6q.10, O1 = dc-gh6q.11, O2 = dc-gh6q.12, C1 = dc-gh6q.13, C2 = dc-gh6q.14.

Owner steps (only the owner performs these; agents prepare scripts and checklists):
1. Approve the AGENTS.md text.
2. Host access, before K1b and S2:
- remove the key the agents' dna-koderbot alias uses from koderbot's authorized_keys, or replace it with an ed25519-sk key with verify-required;
- remove NOPASSWD sudo from koderbot and take it out of the lxd group;
- give the ubuntu admin user a touch-required key and do all sudo and lxc work as that user;
- review whether the proxied service without basic auth should be public.
3. GitHub:
- if the org allows fine-grained tokens, create one for agent sessions limited to DnaCalc/DnaCalc (Contents, Issues and Actions read/write, no Workflows) and switch the agents' gh and git to it;
- push workflow files only with your own credential;
- turn on the org's 2FA requirement;
- with admin:org, read the Actions storage and minutes usage and check that no org runner carries the dnacalc-ci label.
4. Read /sys/block/sda/queue/scheduler. If it is not bfq, set limits.read and limits.write on the profile's root disk.
5. As the admin user:
- `sudo lxd init`: btrfs pool dnacalc (150 GiB loop), lxdbr0 with NAT, no API listener;
- an ACL on lxdbr0 egress rejecting 169.254.0.0/16, RFC 1918 and the host's addresses, except bridge DNS and DHCP;
- host input on lxdbr0 limited to 53 and 67/udp, plus forwarding.
6. Create profile dnacalc-ci from the reviewed yaml. Run `lxc launch ubuntu:24.04 dnacalc-ci -p default -p dnacalc-ci`, push provision.sh and run it as container root.
7. Register the runner at repository level with labels dnacalc-ci and `--disableupdate`, typing the token yourself, then `svc.sh install runner` and start it. Commit deploy/koderbot/identity.json with the container's hostname and machine-id, then `lxc snapshot dnacalc-ci clean`.
8. Upkeep:
- monthly: update the runner and re-snapshot;
- on a schedule and after any suspect run: restore the snapshot;
- health check: `lxc info` plus the runner unit;
- re-register within 14 days of an outage;
- remove the runner before the repository goes public or forking is enabled.

## Do
1. **Profile** (lxd/dnacalc-ci.yaml): privileged false, nesting false, cpu 8, memory 24GiB, swap false, processes 8192, cpu.priority 2, 140GiB root on pool dnacalc, one lxdbr0 NIC, no host disks.
2. **provision.sh** (run as container root). It:
   - installs build-essential, pkg-config, git, curl, python3 and ca-certificates;
   - creates user runner;
   - installs rustup 1.94.1 (minimal, with wasm32, rustfmt and clippy) into root-owned /opt/rust;
   - installs a pinned actions-runner release, checked against a sha256 in the script, into root-owned /opt/actions-runner, with _work and _diag writable by runner;
   - records the sha256s of the binaries in /etc/dnacalc-ci/provisioned.json;
   - installs job-started.sh root-owned and sets ACTIONS_RUNNER_HOOK_JOB_STARTED in the runner's .env.

   It never handles a token.
3. **probe.sh** writes JSON:
   - uid and the result of `sudo -n true`;
   - TCP connects over IPv4 and IPv6 to 127.0.0.1/::1, the bridge gateway and 92.222.230.210 on ports 22, 80, 443, 631, 2019, 8765, 18789 and 18790, plus 169.254.169.254:80;
   - HTTPS to koderbot.dnakode.com and dnacalc.com;
   - host paths, mounts, memory.max, MemTotal, io.max, io.weight and df /;
   - hostname, machine-id and binary hashes against provisioned.json.

   It exits non-zero on any breach or under 20 GiB free.

   PROBE_MEMORY=1 starts a child with oom_score_adj 1000 that stops itself at the constant 26 GiB. The probe refuses to start the child when memory.max is `max` or above 24 GiB, and records that refusal.

   `--cgroup-dir` defaults to /sys/fs/cgroup.
4. **job-started.sh** exits non-zero unless all of these hold:
   - GITHUB_REPOSITORY is DnaCalc/DnaCalc;
   - GITHUB_REF is refs/heads/master;
   - GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF starts with DnaCalc/DnaCalc/.github/workflows/koderbot.yml@refs/heads/master.

   It prints an accept line.
5. **OWNER_STEPS.md**, numbered as this bead's owner actions.
6. **AGENTS.md** (owner-approved text):
   - a third held category: a bead awaiting an owner server, DNS, GitHub-settings or workflow-push step, with a handover note;
   - a build commit that touches .github/workflows is committed but not pushed by the agent, and the owner pushes it, while that clone takes no further bead;
   - agents do not ssh to dna-koderbot;
   - the runner workspace is an allowed full clone;
   - koderbot receipts carry the same trust as ci.yml receipts;
   - the runner is removed before the repository is made public or forking is enabled.

## Port from
fresh

## Acceptance
```text
cargo xtask accept --id dc-gh6q.2 --platform windows --require-all
cargo xtask accept --id dc-gh6q.2 --platform linux --require-all
```
- `dc_gh6q_2__probe_can_fail`: Linux. probe.sh exits non-zero and names each cause for:
- a listener the test opens on 127.0.0.1:18789;
- a temp directory passed as a host path;
- a fake cgroup directory whose memory.max is `max`: the memory child is refused and never started;
- a binary whose hash differs from a temp provisioned.json.

With none of these it exits 0.
- `dc_gh6q_2__hook_refuses_foreign_jobs`: Linux. job-started.sh exits non-zero for another branch, another workflow file, a koderbot.yml path on a branch, and another repository. It exits 0 and prints its accept line for the koderbot.yml-on-master triple.
- `dc_gh6q_2__bounds_agree`: Windows and Linux. The profile's limits.memory plus 2 GiB equals probe.sh's stop constant. The probe's refusal threshold equals limits.memory. provision.sh pins a runner version with a sha256. OWNER_STEPS.md numbers every step the bead lists.
The acceptance runner must verify every named case actually executed and retain the machine-readable receipt.

## Platforms
Windows and Linux. The scripts are POSIX sh, exercised on Linux.

## Out of scope
- koderbot.yml and the live cases (K1b).
- Anything under /srv, Caddy or DNS.
- ci.yml.

## Outcome
deploy/host/dnacalc-site-receive is a Python 3.12+ standard-library forced command. It accepts only these verbs:
- `publish <home|onecalc|calc>`;
- `rollback <site> <release> --if-current <name>`;
- `status <site>`;
- `list <site>`.

It validates each release against its manifest, writes it safely, and switches `current` and `previous` atomically under a lock. It never leaves partial state, never prunes either live release, and logs every action.

sshd-dnacalc-deploy.conf confines user dnacalc-deploy to it.

## Context
Library: Infrastructure (deploy/host).

The owner installs both files (S2); agents never touch the server.

Port from: fresh, recorded in its own section.

Part of epic dc-gh6q; held by dc-oy6l until the owner has ruled on the plan's decisions. Plan keys used in this text: W1 = dc-gh6q.1, K1a = dc-gh6q.2, R1 = dc-gh6q.3, K1b = dc-gh6q.4, K2 = dc-gh6q.5, K3 = dc-gh6q.6, K4 = dc-gh6q.7, S1 = dc-gh6q.8, S2 = dc-gh6q.9, S3 = dc-gh6q.10, O1 = dc-gh6q.11, O2 = dc-gh6q.12, C1 = dc-gh6q.13, C2 = dc-gh6q.14.

Owner steps (only the owner performs these; agents prepare scripts and checklists):
1. Review both files at the build commit.

## Do
1. Receiver:
   - takes the verb only from SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND;
   - reads publish input as uncompressed `r|`, never calling extractall;
   - accepts only regular files whose normalized names are in the manifest, each once: at most 2,000 members and 64 MiB, allowed extensions only, size and sha256 checked;
   - writes with os.open(O_CREAT|O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW, 0o644) into 0755 directories, fsyncs each file and directory, renames <release>.partial, then renames a temporary symlink over `current` and moves the old target to `previous`;
   - takes a non-blocking flock on <root>/<site>/.lock for publish, rollback and prune;
   - ignores SIGHUP, cleans up in finally, and removes stale .partial directories under the lock;
   - keeps five releases, never pruning `current` or `previous`;
   - refuses a rollback whose --if-current has moved;
   - rotates receive.log at 1 MiB, keeping 5 files.
2. sshd drop-in, ending with `Match all`:
   - Match User dnacalc-deploy;
   - AuthorizedKeysFile /etc/ssh/dnacalc-keys/%u;
   - ForceCommand /usr/local/lib/dnacalc/dnacalc-site-receive /srv/dnacalc-site;
   - PermitTTY no; TCP, agent and X11 forwarding off; PermitTunnel no;
   - AuthenticationMethods publickey.

## Port from
fresh

## Acceptance
```text
cargo xtask accept --id dc-gh6q.3 --platform linux --require-all
```
- `dc_gh6q_3__receiver_refuses_unsafe`: Linux, on the receiver file with a temp root. Each of these:
- absolute and `..` paths, a symlink, a hard link, a FIFO and a .sh file;
- a duplicate member, a gzip stream, 10,000 zero-byte members and a 65 MiB stream;
- a wrong sha256 and an unlisted file;
- an unknown site, extra words and shell metacharacters in the command;

exits non-zero, leaves no .partial directory, logs a refusal, and leaves the tree's hash and both symlinks byte-identical.
- `dc_gh6q_3__switch_lock_and_rollback`: Linux:
- During repeated publishes, a reader resolves index.html through `current` and its assets through `current` then `previous`, the lookup order the Caddy files declare (Caddy itself is not run). Every page observed is one complete file set.
- A concurrent second publish is refused.
- A publish killed mid-stream leaves a .partial that the next publish removes.
- A rollback with a stale --if-current is refused, and a correct one restores the release.
- After seven publishes, five releases remain, including `current` and `previous`.
- The drop-in's last line is `Match all`.
The acceptance runner must verify every named case actually executed and retain the machine-readable receipt.

## Platforms
Linux (python3 3.12 or later).

## Out of scope
- Installation (S2).
- The xtask commands (S1).
- Caddy.

## Outcome
Every push to master runs koderbot.yml on the dnacalc-ci runner, separate from ci.yml. The job:
- passes the job-started hook;
- runs probe.sh and `cargo xtask ci linux`;
- uploads koderbot-linux (7-day retention).

The probe proves the isolation in the live run. A memory runaway dies inside the container. No agent session can reach the host with sudo or LXD rights.

## Context
Library: Infrastructure (.github/workflows/koderbot.yml).

Claim this bead only after the owner reports K1a's host-access, GitHub and runner steps done. The build commit touches .github/workflows, so the owner pushes it (K1a's AGENTS.md rule), and the bead is held until then.

ci.yml and ci-audit are unchanged; the artifact is named koderbot-* so no ci.yml audit counts it.

Part of epic dc-gh6q; held by dc-oy6l until the owner has ruled on the plan's decisions. Plan keys used in this text: W1 = dc-gh6q.1, K1a = dc-gh6q.2, R1 = dc-gh6q.3, K1b = dc-gh6q.4, K2 = dc-gh6q.5, K3 = dc-gh6q.6, K4 = dc-gh6q.7, S1 = dc-gh6q.8, S2 = dc-gh6q.9, S3 = dc-gh6q.10, O1 = dc-gh6q.11, O2 = dc-gh6q.12, C1 = dc-gh6q.13, C2 = dc-gh6q.14.

Owner steps (only the owner performs these; agents prepare scripts and checklists):
1. Review and push the koderbot.yml build commit.
2. Dispatch probe_memory once at a quiet hour; agents never dispatch it.

## Do
1. koderbot.yml:
   - push to master, and workflow_dispatch with probe_memory;
   - concurrency group dnacalc-koderbot-${{github.event_name}}, cancel-in-progress false;
   - job koderbot-linux: `if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/master'`, runs-on [self-hosted, linux, x64, dnacalc-ci], permissions contents: read, timeout 120;
   - env: DNACALC_CI_COMMIT, and CARGO_HOME and TMPDIR under runner.temp.
2. Steps:
   - delete _diag files older than 14 days;
   - checkout at ci.yml's pinned SHA with fetch-depth 0 and persist-credentials false;
   - probe.sh into target/ci/koderbot-probe/probe.json (PROBE_MEMORY only on the dispatch input);
   - `cargo xtask ci linux`;
   - upload koderbot-linux (target/ci) with retention-days 7, if: always().

## Port from
fresh

## Acceptance
```text
cargo xtask accept --id dc-gh6q.4 --platform windows --require-all
```
- `dc_gh6q_4__job_isolated_on_koderbot`: Live. ACCEPT-NOT-RUN without DNACALC_KODERBOT_RUN. For that run at HEAD:
- the API job record shows the self-hosted dnacalc-ci runner on refs/heads/master, and the log holds the hook's accept line;
- probe.json shows a non-zero uid, sudo refused, and every listed connect, the metadata address and both HTTPS requests refused;
- no host paths or host disks;
- memory.max equals 24 GiB; io.max is non-empty unless the scheduler is bfq;
- at least 20 GiB free;
- binary hashes match provisioned.json;
- hostname and machine-id equal identity.json;
- `ci-audit --from <artifact> --require-executed-targets linux` exits 0;
- the artifact expires 7 days after creation.
- `dc_gh6q_4__memory_contained`: Live, from the owner's probe_memory dispatch (DNACALC_KODERBOT_MEM_RUN). The child was SIGKILLed at or below 24 GiB, before the 26 GiB constant, and the following `ci linux` step ran.
- `dc_gh6q_4__no_agent_ssh_route`: Windows, from the agents' account. `ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=10` to dna-koderbot and to dna-koderbot-ubuntu either fails, or lands in a shell whose `id -nG` has none of sudo, lxd, adm or caddy and where `sudo -n true` fails.
The acceptance runner must verify every named case actually executed and retain the machine-readable receipt.

## Platforms
Windows: live, read through gh and ssh. The job itself runs in the container.

## Out of scope
- The sweep (K2), issues (K3) and the legacy root (K4).
- Browsers.
- /srv and Caddy.
- ci.yml.

notes:
Provisional plan ID: infra-m0.6. Native export preserves this ID; acceptance command IDs and test names use the same stable plan identity.
Owner ruling (2026-10-18): a copy on GitHub is enough. The bead's "second verified backup on another medium" is dropped. The copy is the evidence repository DnaCalc/OxFunc-Evidence, holding the files in plain git or in Git LFS; that choice follows the measured feasibility check of 2026-10-18 (file types, sizes, compression, GitHub's limits). The rest of the Do stands:
- hash and deduplicate;
- keep versioned manifests;
- restore a random manifest-selected sample from the GitHub copy and check that it hashes identically to the local originals;
- label legacy data without an Excel fingerprint as unknown.
infra_m0_6__restore_roundtrip reconciles the local inventory with the GitHub copy's inventory, not with two backups. Correction to the Do's wording: OxFunc's git now lists smart-fuzzer/cache (1,618 files, 1.4 GB) and smart-fuzzer/runs (26,774 files, 2.0 GB) as ignored, not as untracked and not ignored (git status --ignored, 2026-10-18). The owner has now named the medium, so this bead can be claimed.
Owner note (2026-10-18): the legacy captures can be recreated from Excel, so this backup is a convenience, not a rescue of irreplaceable data. A fresh capture records the Excel build installed at that time, not the builds that made the legacy files. That fits plan 10.2, under which legacy data without an Excel fingerprint is investigation evidence and never certification. Keep the bead proportionate: copy, manifest and a sample restore check, with no extra machinery.
Feasibility check (2026-10-18, read-only workflow wf_73aa1fd8-7da): the copy already exists. DnaCalc/OxFunc-Evidence (private) has held these files since 2026-10-14 (commit e26b7bdd) in plain git:
- one deterministic gzip blob per file, 28,360 files, 2.70 GB raw stored as 261 MB of gzip, with a GitHub pack of about 220 MiB;
- manifest.tsv (raw and gzip SHA-256 for each file) and archive-evidence.py (archive, verify and restore);
- 32 Intel MKL files (746 MB of third-party, re-downloadable binaries) hashed in excluded.tsv but not stored.
A fresh SHA-256 of all 28,392 current files matched the manifest and excluded.tsv: none changed, none new, none missing. Plain git suits this data and LFS is not needed: per-file gzip shrinks the JSON about elevenfold, the largest blob is 15 MB, under GitHub's 100 MiB block, and LFS on the DnaCalc free org plan would meter clone bandwidth past 10 GiB a month. Storing the raw files would not work, because three are over 100 MiB and the total is over the 2 GB push limit.
Remaining Do under the owner's rulings:
- make verify and restore repository commands, with infra_m0_6__restore_roundtrip reconciling the local inventory with the GitHub copy's manifest;
- implement infra_m0_6__unknown_metadata;
- correct the stale wording (the evidence README's second-backup line, and this bead's 'untracked and not ignored' and 'bulk outside git').
322 run files embed absolute C:/Work/DnaCalc/OxFunc/smart-fuzzer paths, and 238 reference other run or cache folders, so a restore keeps the same relative layout under one root.
Sharpened at claim (2026-10-18):
- Files. The repository commands, `cargo xtask evidence verify [--all | --sample N] [--seed S]` and `cargo xtask evidence restore <dir> [--sample N --seed S]`, live in tools/xtask. They locate the evidence checkout at <legacy root>/OxFunc-Evidence and the originals at <legacy root>/OxFunc/smart-fuzzer; DNACALC_LEGACY_ROOT overrides the root, which is how the WSL clone runs. They run archive-evidence.py with python on Windows or python3 on Linux and report its exit code. The record migration/evidence/oxfunc-smart-fuzzer.json holds:
  - the evidence repository URL and the commit DnaCalc relies on;
  - the manifest's SHA-256 and its counts (archived, excluded, raw and gzip bytes);
  - the layout note: 322 run files embed absolute C:/Work paths, and 238 reference other run or cache folders, so a restore keeps the relative layout under one root;
  - one acquisition label per cache subfolder and run folder: a known Excel build with the place it was read from (a path such as cache/oracle/build-20131, or a metadata field of the capture), or unknown, plus the exact target, if any, the entry can certify.
  The evidence README loses the second-backup line and names the xtask commands. Edit it in C:\Work\DnaCalc\OxFunc-Evidence; the orchestrator commits and pushes it. infra-m0.6 is the only bead that may write there.
- Quarantine reading rule (infra-m0.5): labels for run folders that the numeric-provenance record classes as tainted or unresolved (the W108/W109 identification runs, w108a-reference-hunt among them) come from the path only. Their files are not opened. Elsewhere, read only top-level metadata fields (for example a build, version or Excel field), never the capture content for behaviour.
- Case infra_m0_6__restore_roundtrip:
  - the local evidence checkout is clean, and its HEAD equals both the pinned commit and `git ls-remote origin master`, which is not_run with its reason if the network is down;
  - the local inventory of the two directories equals manifest.tsv plus excluded.tsv by path and size;
  - `cargo xtask evidence restore` of a random sample (at least 100 files, seed printed in an ACCEPT-NOTE) yields files byte-identical to the local originals.
  On a host without the evidence checkout or the originals, the case is not_run with that reason, never passed.
- Case infra_m0_6__unknown_metadata: the record's labels match a recomputation from the paths and permitted metadata. A literal known-build example and a literal unknown example appear in the test. Every unknown entry certifies no Exact target, and a known entry certifies only its own build.
- Platforms: Windows, and Linux in the WSL clone with DNACALC_LEGACY_ROOT=/mnt/c/Work/DnaCalc. No new crate dependencies: the case compares bytes and sizes and needs no hashing crate.
- Class: not adversarial. Proportionate, by the owner's note.
Ruling after round 2 (2026-10-18): the round-2 judge's one blocking finding (no bead owned the 3 builds_outside_rule entries) is resolved by an orchestrator step. dc-h9au now owns that decision, and the record's labels_rule.outside_rule cites it. The orchestrator also corrected three comments that said more than the code does: the restore's access to the originals, the path-only files that the restore sample may byte-compare, and the outside-rule text search. Further follow-ups: dc-h9au (per-row capture builds, local-engine records labelled known, inherited files, counts, registering the evidence commands, the evidence README) and dc-1hwy (evidence folders holding copies of tainted files, to be classified by content). No third implementer round, per the owner's proportionate note.
